choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24e48aad-6155-53ef-8a17-f77799abdeeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}+y^{2}=\\frac{4}{121}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717158} {:answer/id #uuid "33a9faa7-f50f-505f-914e-3df3cb2851c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}+y^{2}=\\frac{121}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717160} {:answer/id #uuid "5694fd1f-cdfa-52ed-8e0d-05b06f626427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}+y^{2}=121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717165} {:answer/id #uuid "d7514dc7-5938-5ece-926f-4476efb5d836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}+y^{2}=\\frac{121}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717178} {:answer/id #uuid "f42b8c0a-67b5-5157-a210-5b72a31b39ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}+y^{2}=\\frac{14641}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33a9faa7-f50f-505f-914e-3df3cb2851c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}+y^{2}=\\frac{121}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717160} :answer-field/id #uuid "eae6fc09-0e12-52a4-bd5f-8c19116811da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae8f273a-7873-5a3d-9412-64d30a8b0bd4" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{11}{2}\\sin\\theta,\\quad y=\\frac{11}{2}\\cos\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nDividing each equation by $\\frac{11}{2}$ and squaring gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{121}x^2=\\sin^2\\theta,\\qquad \\frac{4}{121}y^2=\\cos^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nUse $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$ to eliminate $\\theta$. Choose the Cartesian equation." :question/worked-solution "Undo any translations and divide by the common radius to isolate the trigonometric terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\frac{11}{2}\\sin\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{2}{11}x&=\\sin\\theta \\\\ \ny&=\\frac{11}{2}\\cos\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{2}{11}y&=\\cos\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare both normalized equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{121}x^2&=\\sin^2\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{4}{121}y^2&=\\cos^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$, then multiply by $\\frac{121}{4}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{121}x^2+\\frac{4}{121}y^2&=\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{4}{121}\\bigl(x^2+y^2\\bigr)&=1 \\\\ \nx^2+y^2&=\\frac{121}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $x^{2}+y^{2}=\\frac{121}{4}$. The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius is $\\frac{11}{2}$. The full parameter interval traces the entire circle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717194 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1262723e-6804-5009-8bf0-48a0a093fdcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1262723e-6804-5009-8bf0-48a0a093fdcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717188} :answer-field/id #uuid "923a020e-5c30-5206-ab40-8b04924508e1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717201} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "deaa0396-8565-5624-a279-0626a6233d54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "deaa0396-8565-5624-a279-0626a6233d54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717210} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab5a7a8e-dcaa-5347-b24b-41c8f8c41a30" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "663a7a14-999d-5abd-8f40-02b09575681c" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5e14068c370ca692ffcaa52e375dc5fd3892108df5db98a95804ae8d63b9eb2.png)\n\nThe solid arc is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=3\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta$? Use $0\\leq\\theta\\leq2\\pi$ and include both filled endpoints.\n\n$\\theta\\in[$ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $]$" :question/worked-solution "The full circle has equations $x=h+r\\cos\\theta$, $y=k+r\\sin\\theta$, with $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$. Here the radius is $3$ and the center is $(0,0)$.\n\nThe parameter $\\theta$ measures the angle counterclockwise from the rightward horizontal ray through the center. The solid arc starts at $\\theta=\\pi$ and ends at $\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$, following increasing angle. Both endpoints are included.\n\n![](images/94/94853c2bca1ac50aaa4d6fe59d44c66b3a1da1909430b77d322ab34d3e747189.png)\n\nTherefore the required restriction is $\\theta\\in[\\pi,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}]$. The parametric equations of the arc are\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=3\\sin\\theta,\\qquad\\theta\\in[\\pi,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b909f7b5-c7a6-54e1-b2b6-16ea790f1956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b909f7b5-c7a6-54e1-b2b6-16ea790f1956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}